              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 13.7: Layers of the Earth’s atmosphere"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.481,
            0.088,
            0.89,
            0.291
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The Earth’s atmosphere has a layered structure. This structure of the atmosphere is important as it helps explain key weather patterns (Fig. 13.7). Nearly, all weather phenomena take place in the troposphere (with an average height of about 12 km), which is heated from the Earth’s surface. In this layer, temperature decreases with height (~6.5 °C/km). As the warm air rises, it drives winds and storms. The height of the troposphere is maximum above the equator and lowest above the polar regions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.481,
            0.294,
            0.89,
            0.361
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the stratosphere (12 – 50 km), where the ozone layer is located, ozone absorbs UV rays and heats up the atmosphere, so temperature rises with height. This increase"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.066,
            0.369,
            0.235,
            0.386
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meet a Scientist"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.075,
            0.395,
            0.155,
            0.479
          ],
          "content": [
