                0.646
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_6_table_11.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tbody><tr><td>Layer</td><td>Approximate altitudes</td><td>Features</td></tr><tr><td>Troposphere</td><td>0 - 12 km</td><td>Weather formation; temperature decreases with height</td></tr><tr><td>Stratosphere</td><td>12 - 50 km</td><td>Ozone layer absorbs UV; temperature increases with height</td></tr></tbody></table>"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.056,
            0.726,
            0.294,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.072,
            0.761,
            0.275,
            0.902
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Visit the website given below and study the effect of the concentration of greenhouse gas on surface temperature, https://phet.colorado.edu/en/simulations/greenhouse-effect"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.312,
            0.655,
            0.89,
            0.858
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The atmosphere plays two crucial roles in protecting the life on the Earth. Firstly, it partly absorbs the incoming solar radiation. The ozone layer blocks the harmful UV rays. Clouds and other gases also absorb some sunlight before it reaches the surface of the Earth (Fig. 13.5). Secondly, it traps outgoing heat. The Earth’s surface absorbs sunlight and re-radiates it in the infrared region. Greenhouse gases like "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathbb { C O } _ { 2 ^ { \\prime } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " CH and water vapour absorb this re-radiated heat, preventing it from escaping into space. Without the atmosphere, the Earth would be too cold for life to survive. However, excess "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathrm { C O } _ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " from human activities enhances the greenhouse effect, causing global warming, which if left unchecked could make the Earth uninhabitable."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.313,
            0.86,
            0.809,
            0.93
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You have also learnt that Venus is hotter than Mercury, even though Mercury is closer to the Sun. This is because of the presence of an atmosphere on Venus, which has led to an uncontrolled greenhouse effect."
            }
          ]
        },
